{"result":"{
  "id": "18433",
  "key": "SFCC-237",
  "summary": "Spike: MRT Application Architecture Strategy (Single vs Per-Integration MRT Apps)",
  "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issue/18433",
  "description": "**Objective**\nProduce a TSD comparing two approaches for structuring the storefront's MRT (Managed Runtime) Node layer as integrations are built out — **Option A:** a single MRT application responsible for all integrations, vs **Option B:** a dedicated MRT application per integration/domain — so the squad makes a deliberate, signed-off decision on deployment topology before the integration build-out hardens it.\n\n**Background**\nThe storefront currently runs as a single PWA Kit application on MRT (the PWA Kit default), which today isolates upstream integrations only at the network level (per-origin `undici` connection pools). As integration work begins (~18 systems, nearly all not yet started), the number of MRT applications — and how integration/domain code is split across them — becomes a hard-to-retrofit decision affecting deploy/rollback, failure isolation, secrets, cost, and ownership. Tim has asked Angelo (FED) and Gideon (SFCC) to collaborate on this. Complements SFCC-122 (which inventoried what MRT can *consume*); this determines how many MRT apps we *deploy* and how integration work is split across them. The as-is baseline is to be confirmed with Gideon / platform before the options analysis.\n\n**Tasks**\n\n- Confirm the current-state (as-is) baseline with Gideon/platform: single MRT app, proxies wired today, in-process isolation model, the cartridge vs MRT-mediated vs SFCC→MuleSoft integration boundary, deploy/secret model, and platform limits.\n- Confirm whether MRT supports multiple independent applications/projects in our org, and the cost model per environment/app.\n- Define and compare the options — Option A (single app), Option B (per-integration/domain app), and any viable middle option (single modular app) — across deploy/rollback, failure isolation (event loop / memory / process, not just sockets), secrets & security blast radius, performance/LCP, cost, and ownership.\n- Assess the MRT execution/concurrency model and its effect on failure blast radius.\n- Recommend an option with rationale tied to Zimmermann constraints (LCP ≤ 2s, fail-independently, multi-region, squad ownership).\n- Capture the decision in a TSD using the team template; secure Technical Lead sign-off.\n\n**Definition of Done**\nA TSD comparing Option A vs Option B (and any middle option), with an agreed as-is baseline, a recommended option and rationale, and Technical Lead sign-off — sufficient to set the MRT application topology before integration build-out begins.",
  "status": {
    "name": "In Progress",
    "category": "In Progress",
    "color": "yellow"
  },
  "issue_type": {
    "name": "Spike"
  },
  "priority": {
    "name": "Medium"
  },
  "assignee": {
    "display_name": "Marcel Angelo Navarro",
