    "content": {
      "value": "Technical Solution Design — PWA (Architecture)\n==============================================\n\n*Last updated 2026-07-16. Scope: architecture — stack, customisation model, integrations, regional/multi-site, SSR/SEO, performance, accessibility, testing, CI/CD. Requirement IDs (e.g. DMU-030, REG-041) reference the **Zimmermann Functional Requirements** spreadsheet.*\n\n---\n\n1. Purpose & Scope\n------------------\n\nThis TSD describes the technical foundation for the Zimmermann production storefront at <http://www.zimmermann.com> — a luxury Australian fashion brand running on Salesforce Commerce Cloud (SFCC) PWA Kit.\n\n**In scope:**\n\n* Stack and build pipeline\n* The customisation model on top of `@salesforce/retail-react-app`\n* Multi-site / multi-locale routing across 8 storefronts in 6 regions\n* Integration map (SFCC, MuleSoft, Contentful, Global-e, Didomi, Adyen, Omneo, Narvar, GiveX, Google Places)\n* SSR + SEO foundation (DMU-030, hreflang, sitemaps, structured data)\n* Performance budgets, accessibility, consent gating, GDPR\n* Testing strategy and CI/CD topology\n* Open decisions requiring client input before build\n\n**Out of scope:** per-feature functional design; detailed per-vendor integration design\n\n---\n\n2. Solution Overview\n--------------------\n\nA single PWA Kit codebase serves 8 logical SFCC sites, each with its own currency, tax model, locale set, delivery options, payment methods, and consent defaults. International checkout is owned by Global-e; domestic checkout is owned by SFCC. All order-creation, fulfilment write, and CRM sync traffic from SFCC routes through **MuleSoft** as the middleware boundary — SFCC does not call NewStore, AP21, Omneo, or Narvar directly. In addition, the PWA's MRT Node layer calls MuleSoft directly (via `/mobify/proxy/mulesoft/...`) for read operations: order history, order details, boutique stock checks (Find in Boutique, Collect in Boutique), GiveX balance check, and real-time stock availability. These reads bypass SFCC entirely.\n\nThe PWA itself also talks to a handful of services without going through SFCC. Contentful and Didomi are browser-direct. Google Maps (Places API), GiveX balance check (proxied through MuleSoft — see §2 GiveX note), and select MuleSoft operations are called from the PWA's MRT Node layer via proxy, with API credentials held server-side as MRT environment variables.\n\nwide760true ┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Customer browser: │\n│ SSR'd HTML + hydrated React PWA │\n└────────────────┬────────────────┘\n│\n┌─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┐\n│ │ │\nCDN (Akamai / SFCC default) Third-party SaaS APIs (browser handoff\n│ (browser-direct; no SFCC at checkout for\n│ involvement): int'l markets)\n│ ─────────────\nPWA Kit (Managed Runtime) • Contentful Global-e\nReact 18, SSR + CSR • Didomi (CMP) (international\noverrides/ on • Adyen Drop-In (card capture checkout owner)\n@salesforce/retail- + tokenisation at checkout)\nreact-app v10 Post-consent:\n• GTM via Addingwell (loads:\nMRT-proxied (key server-side): Meta, GA4, Pinterest,\n• Google Maps Places TikTok)\n• GiveX balance check • Inside Chat\n(→ MuleSoft → GiveX) • Optimizely Web (US only)\n• MuleSoft (direct calls) • Crazy Egg\n• Booxi (boutique appts) • Narvar Promise (domestic only)\n• reCAPTCHA token verify • Measmerize (PDP)\n(→ Google siteverify) • Vimeo (PDP + editorial)\n• reCAPTCHA v3\n│ ▲\n│ │\n│ │\n│ │\n│ └────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ reads: order history/details, returns │\n│ stock check, GiveX balance check │\n▼ │\n┌──────────────────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Salesforce Commerce Cloud │ │ MuleSoft │\n│ (catalogue, pricebooks, │─────────│ (middleware) │\n│ domestic checkout, BM, │ │ │\n│ GDPR cartridge) │ └────────────────┬───────────────────┘\n│ │ │\n└──────────────┬───────────────┘ ┌────────┬───────┼────────┐\n│ │ │ │ │\n│ NewStore AP21 Omneo Narvar\n▼ OMS (loyalty (CRM→ (EDD,\n┌──────────────┬─────────────────────┐ product Emarsys tracking,\n│ SFCC server-side integrations: │ master; email; returns\n│ • Adyen (domestic payments; │ product/ iFrame) — dom.)\n│ /payments + 3DS server API) │ price feed\n│ • GiveX (validate/redeem/refund │ → SFCC)\n│ via NewStore adapter) │\n│ • Avalara (tax: US dom, NZ, NC, │\n│ EU domestic) │\n│ • Global-e (server-side; NOT via │\n│ MuleSoft; separate from the │\n│ checkout handoff — see legend): │\n│ – FX rates → pricebooks (34..43)│\n│ – catalogue CSV / SFTP (PRC-54) │\n│ – GiveX redeem/refund hooks (44)│\n└────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nLegend / boundaries:\n\n* **PWA → Contentful, Vimeo, Didomi, GTM (via Addingwell), Optimizely Web, Crazy Egg, Inside Chat, Narvar Promise, Adyen Drop-In, Measmerize:** browser-direct; no SFCC involvement. GTM, Optimizely, Crazy Egg, Inside Chat, and Narvar Promise load post-Didomi-consent only.\n* **PWA → Google Maps, GiveX balance check, MuleSoft (select direct calls), Booxi:** MRT-mediated — called from the Node layer via `/mobify/proxy/`; credentials held as MRT environment variables, never in the browser bundle.\n* **PWA → SFCC:** all commerce data (catalogue, cart, pricing, customer, orders) via `@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react`.\n* **SFCC → MuleSoft → NewStore / AP21 / Omneo / Narvar:** write and sync flows initiated by SFCC.\n* **AP21 → MuleSoft → SFCC (inbound):** product and pricing master feed into the SFCC catalogue and pricebooks.\n* **Omneo iFrame (My Account):** also surfaced as a browser-direct iFrame in My Account (CRM-176); all other Omneo traffic via MuleSoft.\n* **PWA (MRT) → MuleSoft → NewStore:** read flows — order history, order details, boutique / BOPIS stock check. SFCC is not in this path.\n* **SFCC server-side integrations (cartridge-based unless noted):** Avalara, Adyen server API, GiveX (via NewStore adapter).\n* **Global-e (two surfaces):** (1) browser handoff iframe at checkout for international orders (PAY-054); (2) server-side pricing/catalogue feed into SFCC (PRC-34..43).\n\n---\n\n3. Frontend Stack\n-----------------\n\n### 3.1 Bundled by PWA Kit\n\n| Layer | Library | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Base framework | PWA Kit on `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10 | Current baseline |\n| UI framework | React 18 + Chakra UI | Component library + theme tokens |\n| Routing | React Router v6 |  |\n| Server / async state | TanStack Query via `@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react` | All SFCC data fetching |\n| i18n | react-intl | Translations under `translations/` |\n| Build / dev | Webpack 5 via `pwa-kit-dev` |  |\n\n### 3.2 Project additions\n\n| Layer | Library | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Type safety | TypeScript strict mode | All custom code is `.ts` / `.tsx` |\n| Component explorer | Storybook 8 + `@storybook/addon-a11y` | `play` functions for interaction tests |\n| Unit testing | Vitest (+ `vitest-axe`) | Project-level test runner |\n| E2E | Playwright (+ `axe-playwright`) | Multi-region fixtures from day one |\n| Forms | react-hook-form + zod | Introduced when checkout / account work begins |\n| Global UI state | Zustand | Per-request store in SSR |\n\n---\n\n4. Customisation Model\n----------------------\n\nThis project uses the Salesforce PWA Kit's *template extensibility* model. `@salesforce/retail-react-app` is consumed as an unmodified npm dependency, not a forked copy of the repository.\n\nAll customisations live in an `overrides/` directory that mirrors the structure of the underlying `@salesforce/retail-react-app` package. The base package is consumed unmodified — overrides take precedence at resolution time. This keeps the project upgradeable: pulling a new Salesforce baseline is a dependency bump plus a targeted reconciliation of overridden files, not a full re-application of patches.\n\n**Working principle:** stay as close to base PWA Kit behaviour as possible. Visual changes (Chakra props, theme tokens, SCSS where Chakra is insufficient) are expected. Structural changes (data flow, routing, or layout deviations from the base template) are taken deliberately and reviewed against the upgrade impact before being merged.\n\n**Brand-new components** (with no base equivalent) will also live in the `overrides/` folder.\n\n### 4.1 Environment Configuration\n\nAll secrets and per-tier identifiers are injected as **Managed Runtime environment variables**. Unless public identifiers, they must never be committed to source control or exposed in client-side bundles. The canonical list of every variable the storefront expects is maintained in the project's environment-variable template, grouped by integration with inline comments — that template is the single source of truth. The table below shows the groups so each credential's boundary is clear.\n\n| Group | Purpose | Boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `COMMERCE_API_*`, `EINSTEIN_API_SITE_ID` | SFCC SCAPI / SLAS client config | Browser-direct (public client ID) + MRT-mediated (token refresh) |\n| `GOOGLE_CLOUD_API_KEY` | Google Maps Places API (address autocomplete) | MRT-mediated — key never reaches the browser |\n| `CONTENTFUL_SPACE_ID`, `CONTENTFUL_ENVIRONMENT_ID`, `CONTENTFUL_DELIVERY_TOKEN` | Contentful Delivery API (published content) | Browser + SSR. CDA token is read-only and scoped to one environment; safe in bundle but held as an MRT env var so rotation is centralised |\n| `CONTENTFUL_PREVIEW_TOKEN`, `CONTENTFUL_HOST` | Contentful Preview API (drafts, editor sessions) | MRT-mediated — proxied server-side; never reaches the browser |\n| `CONTENTFUL_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN` | Contentful Management API (schema sync) | **Local / CI only** — never deployed to MRT |\n| `CMS_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | Shared secret for the Contentful → MRT cache-purge webhook (HMAC verification) | MRT-mediated |\n| `MULESOFT_BASE_URL`, `MULESOFT_CLIENT_ID`, `MULESOFT_CLIENT_SECRET` | PWA → MuleSoft direct calls (`/mobify/proxy/mulesoft/...`): order history, order details, boutique / BOPIS stock, GiveX balance | MRT-mediated — credentials never reach the browser |\n| `ADYEN_CLIENT_KEY`, `ADYEN_ENVIRONMENT` | Adyen Drop-In / Components SDK at checkout (tokenisation, 3DS2 inline). Server-side `/payments` calls and HMAC live in SFCC BM | Browser-direct — client key is public by design (PAY-021) |\n| `INSIDE_CHAT_SITE_TOKEN` | Inside Chat support widget. **Likely NOT PWA-owned** — Inside Chat loads via GTM script injection through the Addingwell container (HPC-039/040), so its token sits inside the GTM container config (Tell No Lies), not a PWA env var. Retain only if the PWA must initialise the widget itself; confirm with Tell No Lies | Browser-direct — public token (if used at all) |\n| `NARVAR_ACCOUNT_ID` | Narvar Promise (formerly CONVERT) EDD widget at checkout (domestic only) | Browser-direct — public ID |\n| `RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY` | reCAPTCHA v3 site key (registration / login forms) | Browser-direct — public site key only |\n| `RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY` | reCAPTCHA v3 secret key — used by the MRT layer to verify tokens against Google's `siteverify` endpoint; never reaches the browser | MRT-mediated |\n| `GLOBALE_*` | Global-e merchant ID, environment, script host, supported countries, server-side API key | Browser-direct (script host, merchant ID) + MRT-mediated (API key, if used) — final shape pending PRC-34..43 sign-off |\n| `DIDOMI_API_KEY` | Didomi Notice/Consent ID | Browser-direct — public ID, safe in bundle |\n| `GTM_CONTAINER_ID`, `GTM_SERVER_URL` | GTM container and Addingwell first-party tagging-server hostname (`<https://ruffles.zimmermann.com`).> The loader uses both: `${GTM_SERVER_URL}/gtm.js?id=${GTM_CONTAINER_ID}` | Browser-direct — both public, safe in bundle. One pair per environment |\n| `SENTRY_DSN`, `NEW_RELIC_LICENSE_KEY`, `ENVIRONMENT_NAME` | Observability | MRT-mediated (license keys held server-side); `ENVIRONMENT_NAME` (one of `local`, `dev`, `sit`, `stage`, `uat`, `prod`) is the per-tier tag emitted to Sentry / New Relic / GTM |\n| `MARKETING_CLOUD_*`, `COMMERCE_AGENT_SETTINGS`, `OTP_TOKEN_LENGTH` | PWA Kit base-template defaults referenced by the base runtime configuration | Likely removable once email/auth wiring is finalised (Zimmermann uses Emarsys via Omneo, not Marketing Cloud) |\n\n**Security policy:**\n\n* Real values live only in local, untracked environment files — one shared file (`.env.shared`, tier-independent) plus one per deployment tier (`.env.<tier>` for dev/test/sit/uat/staging/prod), managed via `npm run env` (`scripts/switchEnv.mjs`). Only a template — variable names tagged `# scope:shared|tier|personal|auto` on their own comment line, no secret values — is committed to source control as `.env.sample`. Two guardrails enforce this: a pre-commit hook that blocks commit if `.env.sample` contains a secret-shaped non-empty value, and a Claude Code rule that instructs the AI assistant never to write real secret values into `.env.sample`.\n* Public identifiers (e.g. the GTM container ID, Didomi notice ID, SFCC SLAS client ID) may carry placeholder values in the committed template.\n* Secrets (tokens, license keys, HMAC secrets, server-side API keys) appear in the committed template as the variable name only, with an empty value.\n* Non-personal shared/tier values can be centrally published and pulled via this repo's GitHub Environments (`shared` + one per deployment tier under Settings > Environments), stored as GitHub Environment Variables — write access requires repo Admin (`@ZimmermannAU/tier-1-approvers`), read access is repo-collaborator-wide. This is a deliberate, documented exception to using GitHub Secrets (which cannot be read back via API once set) — see `.env.sample`'s header for the full rationale. Genuinely personal credentials (`scope:personal` — your own Jira/Confluence token, your own Contentful CMA token) are always excluded from this and never centralized.\n* MRT variables are managed per environment (Dev / SIT / Stage / UAT / Prod) in the Managed Runtime console and rotated independently of the codebase.\n\n---\n\n5. Multi-Site, Multi-Locale Architecture\n----------------------------------------\n\n### 5.1 Storefronts\n\n**8 independent SFCC sites across 6 regions**, each with its own currency, tax model, locale set, delivery options, and payment methods. 11 active locales total. US/EU domestic and international are configured as separate SFCC sites (REG-006).\n\n| Locale | URL prefix | Currency | Checkout owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| AU + Oceania | `/` (x-default hreflang) | AUD | SFCC (REG-003) |\n| UK | `/uk/` | GBP | SFCC (REG-004) |\n| US domestic | `/us/` | USD | SFCC (REG-005) |\n| US international | `/en-ca/` | USD | Global-e (REG-005) |\n| EU domestic — EN / FR / IT / ES | `/en-eu/`, `/fr-eu/`, `/it-eu/`, `/es-eu/` | EUR | SFCC (REG-006 dom) |\n| EU international | `/en-ch/` | EUR | Global-e (REG-006 intl) |\n| HK | `/hk/` | HKD (Global-e display) | Global-e (REG-007) |\n| ME | `/en-ae/` | AED (Global-e display) | Global-e (REG-008) |\n\nRest-of-world traffic routes via `/en-ch/` and `/en-ca/` (DMU-013). AU is configured at the root with no locale prefix and serves as the x-default hreflang target (DMU-016).\n\n### 5.2 Entry routing\n\n* **IP geolocation** selects the site on first visit (REG-013).\n* **AU is gated** to Oceania IPs only (REG-009) but remains the x-default hreflang target, so gating runs in the application layer rather than at the network. Non-Oceania human visitors are redirected server-side to their matched storefront before AUD pricing renders; verified search-engine and social-unfurler bots are exempt; UA ambiguity fails open (false positives have no SEO cost; missing a real crawler does).\n* **Cross-region entry handling for all other storefronts** — first-visit mismatch-detection slide-out, the country/language selector, and the EU language-mismatch modal — is functional design (REG-014–035), out of scope for this TSD.\n\n### 5.3 Single source of truth for country / locale\n\nCountry, locale, currency, shipping country, Global-e flag, and boutique-region data live in a single front-end configuration module. When a market is added, that module and the SFCC site configuration are updated together.\n\n### 5.4 Per-storefront concerns that must not bleed\n\n* **Consent defaults (REG-054):** per-storefront Didomi defaults are site-scoped config that must not bleed across storefronts; the values themselves are specified under REG-054.\n* **Tax model:** SFCC native tax tables (AU GST, UK VAT); Avalara for US domestic, NZ, New Caledonia, and EU domestic (EU domestic added 2026-06-19); Global-e handles tax for international checkout paths.\n* **Marketing opt-in default (REG-057):** site-scoped per storefront; must not bleed — value specified under REG-057.\n* **Payment methods:** per-storefront Adyen configuration. Apple Pay, Google Pay, and Klarna are post-launch.\n\n### 5.5 Session and basket scope across storefronts\n\nAll eight storefronts are path-prefixed on a single first-party domain and run on a single SFCC realm with shared customer data. This determines how a shopper's session behaves when they cross storefronts (via the country / language selector or the mismatch slide-out):\n\n* **Session cookie is shared.** The `httpOnly` SLAS refresh-token cookie set by the SSR layer is first-party to the one domain, so it is presented on requests to any storefront prefix — no cross-domain SSO bridge is needed.\n* **Tokens are channel-scoped.** A SLAS token carries the site it was issued for, so the SSR layer re-initialises the SLAS context for the destination storefront. Because the customer record is shared across the realm, a registered shopper is silently re-recognised without re-entering credentials; a guest session is site-scoped and does not carry over.\n* **Basket does not transfer — by design.** Baskets are site-scoped and priced in the storefront's own currency against its own pricebook, so a shopper switching storefront starts a fresh basket. Carrying a basket across a currency / pricebook boundary would produce incorrect pricing.\n* **Checkout handoff is separate.** When an international storefront hands off to Global-e, the SLAS session does not extend into the Global-e hosted checkout — Global-e owns that session in full.\n\n---\n\n6. Checkout Ownership Branching\n-------------------------------\n\nCheckout owner is determined at PDP / cart, not only at the checkout page — so price, shipping, duty, and tax presentation are correct before the customer commits.\n\n* **SFCC owns:** AU domestic (including Oceania — Avalara for NZ + New Caledonia), UK, US (when shipping to US), EU domestic.\n* **Global-e owns:** HK, ME, EU international, the US site when shipping outside the US, and any domestic storefront when the selected shipping destination is outside that storefront's market.\n* **Pricing for Global-e markets (REG-041):** SFCC holds all pricebooks (including Global-e ones), derived nightly from AP21 base prices and Global-e FX/coefficients. SFCC sends the complete cart in target currency at handoff; Global-e owns shipping, duties, and tax from that point. Full mechanism specified under PRC-34..43.\n* **Global-e shipping country lock (REG-028):** locked at the country selector pre-checkout; cannot change once inside Global-e.\n\n---\n\n7. Integration Map\n------------------\n\n### 7.1 Classification model\n\nEvery integration is placed in one of five **boundary classes** based on where its network call originates. The split is determined by the vendor's documented auth model — a publishable key implies browser-direct is safe; a secret key forces a server-side hop.\n\n* **Browser-direct** — call originates from the user's browser. Vendor publishes a publishable/public key designed to ship in the JS bundle (with referrer or origin restrictions where applicable). No secret material is ever exposed.\n* **MRT-mediated** — call originates from the Node layer that hosts PWA Kit (the SSR pass, a server-only proxy endpoint in the SSR/Node layer, the `/mobify/proxy/` forwarding layer, or a webhook receiver). Used whenever the vendor requires a secret token, whenever an API key must be kept off the browser bundle entirely, or whenever `httpOnly` cookies must be set or read. Credentials are held as MRT environment variables. This includes calls the PWA makes directly to MuleSoft via MRT proxy (rather than routing through SFCC).\n* **SFCC-cartridge** — call originates from inside Salesforce Commerce Cloud (a Demandware cartridge). The PWA never makes the call; credentials live in SFCC Business Manager.\n* **via MuleSoft** — SFCC-side, server-to-server through the MuleSoft middleware. The PWA never sees the downstream system.\n* **Out-of-band** — runs outside the storefront runtime entirely (scheduled feed, build-time pipeline, or system-to-system integration that bypasses the PWA and often SFCC too).\n\nA single integration can span multiple classes — e.g. Adyen is `SFCC-cartridge` for `/payments` calls and `Browser-direct` for the Drop-In SDK; Global-e is `Browser-direct` (checkout handoff — iframe embed) and `SFCC-cartridge` (pricing feed); GiveX is `via MuleSoft` / server-side NewStore-adapter for issue/validate/redeem/refund and `MRT-mediated` for balance checks. Compound rows list every class that applies.\n\n> Reading the table: **Call origin** = where the network request is issued from. **Hosting layer** = which boundary class above. **Security** = how credentials are exposed.\n\n### 7.2 Integration map\n\n| System | Role | Call origin | Hosting layer | Security |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **SFCC (SCAPI)** | Storefront, catalogue, basket, customer, orders, BM config, domestic checkout | Browser (Shopper API via `commerce-sdk-react`) + MRT (token refresh, OCAPI residue) | Browser-direct + MRT-mediated | SLAS client ID public in bundle; SLAS client secret on MRT for the public-client PKCE flow; refresh tokens set as `httpOnly` cookies by the Node layer, never visible to JS |\n| **Contentful — Delivery (CDA)** | Published editorial content (on live website) | MRT + Browser | Browser-direct + MRT-mediated | CDA token is read-only and scoped to one environment; safe in bundle. SSR-first for SEO/LCP, browser hydration uses the SSR payload |\n| **Contentful — Preview (CPA)** | Editor preview sessions, draft content | MRT (SSR pass and editor proxy) | MRT-mediated | CPA token is semi-sensitive (exposes drafts); held server-side and proxied through MRT — never reaches the browser |\n| **Contentful — Management (CMA)** | Schema sync (`npm run sync:contentful`), Studio editor OAuth | Out-of-band (CI host) | Out-of-band | CMA token never embedded; stored as a CI environment variable on the host that runs the sync |\n| **Contentful publish webhook** | Cache invalidation on entry publish | Contentful → MRT | MRT-mediated | Webhook receiver in the Node/SSR layer; shared-secret HMAC verification on the incoming request |\n| **Vimeo** | Product video hosting — PDP gallery, runway / campaign clips (PRD-066/067) | Browser (lazy iframe embed) | Browser-direct | Embed URL stored in product data or the CMS; no secret. Loaded behind a facade (poster image + click/scroll-to-load) so the Vimeo player JS stays off the LCP critical path. Editorial-page video is Contentful by default but may also embed Vimeo by URL (HPC-004). PRD-067 (Vimeo as the chosen platform) still Open |\n| **Didomi** | Consent management; gates every third-party script | Browser | Browser-direct | Public Notice/Consent ID in bundle; no secret. SDK script loaded first-party. Where consent cannot be determined, **fail closed** — no third-party scripts load |\n| **Google Maps (Places API)** | Address autocomplete at checkout (delivery and billing — CFD-023, CFD-030, CFD-059) | Browser → MRT | MRT-mediated | API key held as MRT environment variable; never reaches the browser bundle. Graceful degradation to manual entry if the API is unavailable |\n| **GTM (hosted on Addingwell)** | Tag orchestration, data-layer consumer, Consent Mode v2, Enhanced Conversions; loads downstream tags (Meta, Pinterest, TikTok, GA4, etc.) via Tell No Lies. **Optimizely Web and Crazy Egg are NOT in the GTM container** (confirmed 09-06, AAB-004/005) — separate front-end head tags, see rows below | Browser, post-consent | Browser-direct (Addingwell first-party hostname) | Container ID and Addingwell tagging-server URL public in bundle. The container is hosted on Addingwell (Didomi-owned), so the browser fetches the loader from a Zimmermann first-party subdomain (Addingwell custom path, not the vanilla `/gtm.js` from `googletagmanager.com`). Tell No Lies owns the container and the Addingwell workspace; Accenture's responsibility ends at emitting the data layer and pointing the loader at the Addingwell hostname. Hashed email passed at order confirmation for Enhanced Conversions |\n| **Optimizely Web** | A/B testing & experimentation (AAB-004) | Browser, post-consent | PWA-direct front-end head tag | **Not a GTM tag.** PWA injects the Optimizely snippet directly in the page head **before render** (anti-flicker). **US storefront only; one snippet for all environments.** Zimmermann provides the script. Didomi-gated. SFCC emits the data-layer segmentation attributes per AAB-002 |\n| **Crazy Egg** | Heatmapping & session replay (AAB-005) | Browser, post-consent | PWA-direct front-end head tag | **Not a GTM tag.** PWA injects the Crazy Egg snippet directly in the page head — loaded async and strictly post-Didomi-consent, so it never blocks first paint. Didomi-gated. SFCC emits data-layer attributes (page type, authenticated state, storefront region) per AAB-002 |\n| **Inside Chat** | Customer support widget | Browser, post-Didomi consent | PWA-direct | **Not a GTM tag.** PWA loads the Inside Chat script directly in the page head — async and post-Didomi-consent, so it never blocks first paint (HPC-039/040). Site token (`INSIDE_CHAT_SITE_TOKEN`) is public in bundle. Didomi consent category TBC. Page visibility rules owned by Zimmermann. |\n| **Adyen — Drop-In / Components** | Card capture, 3DS inline, PayPal redirect, tokenisation at checkout | Browser | Browser-direct | Adyen client key public in bundle; tokenises card data so raw PAN never reaches Zimmermann systems (PAY-021). 3DS handled inline — no redirect (PAY-045) |\n| **Adyen — server API** | `/payments`, `/payments/details`, notifications, refunds | SFCC server | SFCC-cartridge | No PWA involvement |\n| **Global-e — checkout handoff** | International checkout owner; SFCC sends complete cart in target currency at handoff (PRC-16, PRC-43) | Browser iframe | Browser-direct | Iframe embed on a Zimmermann checkout page — shopper stays on `zimmermann.com` (PAY-054). Cart payload signed/prepared server-side by SFCC; Global-e owns the entire session from handoff (shipping, duties, tax, payment, confirmation email — CFD-071). Shipping country locked at the country selector pre-checkout (REG-028) |\n| **Global-e — server-side feed** | Nightly FX / coefficients → SFCC pricebooks (PRC-34..43); daily catalogue CSV via SFTP (PRC-54); GiveX validate/redeem/refund webhooks exposed to Global-e (PRC-44) | SFCC server / SFTP | SFCC-cartridge + Out-of-band | No PWA involvement |\n| **Narvar — Promise widget** | Domestic EDD displayed alongside each delivery method at checkout (DLV-009, CFD-050) | Browser, post-consent | Browser-direct | Account ID public; widget loads its own script. Suppress widget on failure — never block checkout |\n| **Narvar — tracking / returns / email** | Post-purchase tracking portal, returns portal, order confirmation / shipping emails (domestic only) | SFCC server → MuleSoft → Narvar | via MuleSoft | No PWA involvement — Narvar backend credentials in MuleSoft. International tracking goes to Global-e instead (OM-020) |\n| **Avalara** | Tax for US domestic, NZ, New Caledonia, and EU domestic | SFCC server during pricing | SFCC-cartridge | EU domestic added 2026-06-19. No PWA involvement. |\n| **GiveX — issue / validate / redeem / refund** | Gift card validate, redeem, and refund at checkout (PWA → SCAPI/SFCC → NewStore adapter → GiveX); validate/redeem/refund webhooks exposed to Global-e (PRC-44); gift-card issuance at purchase (SCAPI/SFCC → MuleSoft → NewStore → GiveX) | SFCC server | via MuleSoft / NewStore adapter | No PWA involvement |\n| **GiveX — balance check** | Real-time balance check in My Account and at checkout entry (GC-037, GC-053); available on domestic and international storefronts | Browser → MRT → MuleSoft → GiveX | MRT-mediated (HTTPS to MuleSoft) | GiveX is reachable only over a persistent TCP socket, so MuleSoft (not MRT) owns the GiveX connection and credentials. MRT exposes an HTTPS endpoint, holds the MuleSoft credential as an env var, and applies rate-limit / bot-protection on the route. The request carries card number and PIN — both payment credentials, POST body only, never logged. PWA sees only a typed JSON response (balance, currency, expiry, masked card number) |\n| **Google reCAPTCHA** | Bot protection on registration / login | Browser (site key + token collection) + MRT (token verification via Google `siteverify`) | Browser-direct + MRT-mediated | Site key (`RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY`) public in bundle. Secret key (`RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY`) held as an MRT environment variable; never reaches the browser — MRT calls Google's `siteverify` endpoint to verify the token before forwarding to SCAPI. Loads on register / login form interaction; **consent-gated in EU & UK only** (post-Didomi-consent; no bot protection where consent is declined), elsewhere loads regardless of consent. See §11.1 |\n| **GDPR cartridge** | DSAR export, right-to-erasure, data retention (REG-060–067) | SFCC server | SFCC-cartridge | OOTB cartridge; flows entirely server-side. PWA touchpoint limited to surfacing the entry point in My Account (§11.3). On erasure, Adyen tokens are also deleted |\n| **MuleSoft** | Middleware. All SFCC ↔ OMS / loyalty / CRM / EDD / booking calls route through it (CAI-022). Select PWA operations also call MuleSoft directly via MRT proxy (`/mobify/proxy/mulesoft/...`) rather than routing through SFCC | SFCC server (SFCC→MuleSoft flows) + MRT (PWA→MuleSoft via proxy) | via MuleSoft + MRT-mediated | SFCC→MuleSoft: mTLS / API key; credentials in SFCC BM and MuleSoft config. PWA→MRT→MuleSoft: `client_id` + `client_secret` (MuleSoft platform credentials) held as MRT environment variables; `customer_id` passed as a plain UUID query parameter. |\n| **NewStore OMS — writes** | Order creation, carrier selection, dispatch, label generation, BOPIS fulfilment; **system of record for order history** (DMU-007 / 008) | SFCC server → MuleSoft → NewStore | via MuleSoft | No PWA involvement — credentials in MuleSoft; SFCC initiates all write flows |\n| **NewStore OMS — reads** | Order history (CRM-056–057), order details (CRM-071–072), boutique / BOPIS stock check (CFD-043), real-time stock availability | MRT → MuleSoft → NewStore | MRT-mediated | MRT authenticates the channel to MuleSoft using `client_id` + `client_secret` (MRT environment variables); `customer_id` passed as a plain UUID query parameter. SFCC is not in the read path. All NewStore API credentials held in MuleSoft |\n| **AP21** | Retail Rewards balance, product master, composition / material data, HS codes for Avalara; **source of product and pricing master data** | SFCC server → MuleSoft → AP21 (real-time call at login for Rewards balance); AP21 → MuleSoft → SFCC (inbound product + pricing feed into the catalogue and pricebooks) | via MuleSoft | No direct PWA call |\n| **Omneo** | CRM / identity & loyalty system of record. SFCC ↔ Omneo bidirectional via MuleSoft webhooks (CFD-012, CRM-010, CRM-011); Omneo → Emarsys for email | SFCC server ↔ MuleSoft ↔ Omneo, plus an Omneo iFrame in My Account writing directly to Omneo (CRM-176) | via MuleSoft + Browser-direct (iFrame) | MuleSoft holds Omneo API credentials. iFrame auth mechanism TBC. **Load-bearing** for My Account profile pages (DMU-004) — fail with explicit retry UI, never render partial data |\n| **Emarsys** | Email marketing | Omneo → Emarsys (server-to-server) | Out-of-band (downstream of Omneo) | No PWA involvement |\n| **Booxi** | Boutique appointment booking (My Account; CRM-165–167) | PWA → MRT → Booxi (direct) | MRT-mediated | Booxi credentials held as MRT environment variables |\n| **Transperfect** | Translation pipeline for Contentful (REG-047) | Contentful ↔ Transperfect directly | Out-of-band | No PWA involvement |\n| **Google Business API** | Daily store-hours feed (boutique opening hours) | Scheduled job → SFCC | Out-of-band | No PWA involvement |\n| **Feedonomics** | Product feed for downstream marketing channels (PRD-071) | Scheduled feed out of SFCC | Out-of-band | No PWA involvement |\n| **Measmerize** | Find My Size widget + size guide overlay on PDP (PRD-050–053). The PWA injects the Measmerize script on PDP and passes the product identifier. The widget self-determines whether it has data for the product and signals the PWA accordingly — the PWA either shows the Find My Size trigger or falls back to the Contentful size guide. Widget configuration, size guide content, and recommendation logic are owned by Measmerize / Zimmermann. | Browser (PDP only) | Browser-direct | Public identifier in bundle; no secret. Script defers behind LCP. **Consent approach unconfirmed — open item.** |\n| **Shippit / Ordoro** | Carrier label automation | NewStore ↔ Shippit / Ordoro; SFCC does not call them | Out-of-band | No PWA involvement |\n\n### 7.3 Contentful schema as code\n\nContent type definitions live in version control and are deployed to Contentful via an idempotent sync script (`npm run sync:contentful`) using a Contentful Management Token. The script is field-additive — it will not delete fields from a live content type. Each definition pairs with a typed React component, so schema changes are reviewable and repeatable. Experience Builder components (drag-and-drop in Contentful Studio) are registered through a separate registration module.\n\n### 7.4 Non-system stakeholders\n\nParties that influence the build but are not systems the storefront integrates with at runtime. Their deliverables hand over to Accenture for implementation.\n\n| Party | Role | Handover to Accenture |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Sparro** | External SEO consultancy. Defines the JSON-LD schema templates (PDP, PLP, boutique, homepage — DMU-037 to DMU-040), the consolidated hreflang structure (DMU-025), and the CMS-editable on-page SEO field schema (DMU-035). | Specification documents per requirement ID; Accenture implements in SFCC / PWA. Final JSON-LD scope and page types to be confirmed before build (DMU-038). |\n| **Accessibility vendor** *(TBC by Zimmermann)* | Manual accessibility audits, screen-reader and assistive-tech testing, audit cadence, remediation triage. Complements the automated coverage in §10. | Audit reports per cadence; defects raised back into the Accenture backlog. Vendor name, screen-reader / browser matrix, and cadence still pending from Zimmermann. |\n| **Tell No Lies** | Zimmermann's marketing technology partner. Owns the GTM container configuration and the downstream tags loaded inside GTM (GA4, Meta, Pinterest, TikTok, etc.). The container is hosted on **Addingwell** (Didomi-owned managed tagging server) — Tell No Lies operates the Addingwell workspace, not just the GTM container. **Optimizely Web and Crazy Egg** are loaded as PWA-direct head tags; Optimizely's script is supplied by Zimmermann. | Accenture's responsibility ends at emitting a comprehensive data layer (AAB-001 / AAB-002), embedding the GTM container ID + Addingwell tagging-server URL, and injecting the Optimizely/Crazy Egg head snippets (Didomi-gated). Tell No Lies consumes the data layer and configures the GTM-housed tags. |\n\n---\n\n8. SSR, SEO, and GEO Foundation\n-------------------------------\n\n### 8.1 SSR (DMU-030)\n\nAll product, category, and content pages will use SSR for full crawlability by search engines and AI bots without dependence on client-side JS. The PWA Kit SSR pipeline is preserved end-to-end. Componentry that cannot SSR is rendered through a hydration-safe boundary that still emits SEO-relevant markup on the server. Global state stores are initialised per request to prevent state leaking across SSR responses.\n\n#### 8.1.1 SSR vs CSR by page type\n\nPWA Kit always runs the SSR pipeline — every page is server-rendered to some degree. The practical distinction is what content is rendered on the server vs fetched after hydration on the client.\n\n| Page type | Pattern | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| PDP, PLP, content pages, homepages, boutique pages | **Full SSR** — markup and data rendered on the server | Indexable, SEO- and LCP-critical |\n| Cart, checkout, My Account, order history, wishlist, address book | **SSR shell + CSR data** — server renders the layout, header, footer, and skeleton; `@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react` hydrates personalised data (basket lines, customer profile, orders) client-side after hydration | Personalised, per-session, behind authentication. No SEO value (blocked in `robots.txt` — §8.3). Server-rendering personalised data would defeat edge caching and risk state leaking across requests |\n| Search results, filtered PLP states | **SSR shell + CSR data** | Blocked in `robots.txt`; filter / sort state is client-driven |\n\nThe \"SSR shell + CSR data\" pattern still gives the user instant first paint of the page chrome — only the personalised payload waits on hydration. No page is purely client-rendered.\n\n### 8.2 Canonicals & hreflang\n\nCanonical tags on every indexable page; filtered PLPs canonicalise to the unfiltered base PLP. hreflang on canonical PDPs only, delivered via XML sitemap, consolidated to ~12 regional/language-level tags. Detailed canonical/hreflang rules are specified in the functional requirements.\n\n### 8.3 Sitemaps & robots\n\nPer-storefront / per-locale XML sitemaps refreshed daily, excluding disabled and deleted pages (DMU-026). `robots.txt` permits indexable pages (including the bag page) and blocks checkout, account, search results, and filtered URL parameters. URLs blocked in `robots.txt` never appear in a sitemap.\n\n### 8.4 Structured data\n\nCustom JSON-LD per the Sparro templates, rendered server-side so crawlers see it on the first response:\n\n* **PDP:** Product + Merchant Listing Schema (`ProductGroup` + variant `Product`s), with real-time inventory driving availability.\n* **PLP:** CollectionPage + BreadcrumbList + ItemList Schema (ItemList reflects the initial SSR'd product batch).\n* **Boutique pages:** LocalBusiness / ClothingStore Schema per location.\n* **Homepages:** Organisation + WebSite Schema per regional storefront, with per-prefix `WebSite` nodes (including the Global-e international paths `/en-ca/` and `/en-ch/`).\n\n### 8.5 URL strategy\n\n* Product URL: `/[prefix]/[name]-[colour].html` — the `.html` suffix is preserved to maintain existing indexed URLs.\n* Category URL: `/[prefix]/[slug]` (no suffix), with 301 redirects from legacy paths.\n* 301 redirects for every URL that changes as a result of migration (products, categories, content). Implemented at the CDN/edge layer to keep application-layer impact minimal.\n\n### 8.6 GEO (DMU-042)\n\nNo GEO-specific features in launch scope. The technical foundations enabling post-launch GEO — SSR, structured data, canonicals, XML sitemaps — are in place at go-live so no architecture decision blocks future activation.\n\n---\n\n9. Performance Strategy\n-----------------------\n\nLCP at p75 mobile is the binding constraint and the de-facto cap on third-party script weight.\n\n| Metric | Target |\n| --- | --- |\n| LCP (mobile p75) | ≤ 2 s |\n| Lighthouse Performance | ≥ 0.80 |\n| Lighthouse Accessibility | ≥ 0.90 |\n| Lighthouse Best Practices | ≥ 0.90 |\n\n### 9.1 Third-party defer rule\n\nAnything loaded via Didomi callback (GTM — which loads Meta, GA4, and other GTM-housed tags via Tell No Lies; Narvar EDD widget; Inside Chat) is deferred, async-loaded, or injected post-hydration so it does not block LCP. **Measmerize** (Find My Size / size guide — PDP only) likewise defers behind LCP — the script injects after the product hero image has loaded. **reCAPTCHA** likewise loads only on register/login form interaction, so it never sits on the critical path of any other page (consent treatment per §11.1). **Exception (AAB-004):** Optimizely Web is a PWA-direct head tag (not a GTM tag) and deliberately loads in the page head **before render** to prevent A/B flicker, scoped to the US storefront where Optimizely runs. **Crazy Egg (AAB-005)** loads async and post-Didomi-consent. Both load strictly post-Didomi-consent; keep both snippets minimal.\n\n### 9.2 Other levers\n\n* **Images:** all storefront imagery — product and editorial — is stored in and served from **Contentful** (`images.ctfassets.net`) via the Contentful Images API; SFCC holds no image assets (DMU-010 migration de-scoped; product images CRM-075; all media via the CMS library HPC-003). The PWA generates `srcset` / `sizes` and sets size, quality, and format per breakpoint on the asset URL — **AVIF primary with WebP / JPEG fallbacks**. Lazy-load all non-LCP images; priority-load the primary hero image (PRD-064). Never render full-resolution images at thumbnail size.\n* **Video:** sourced by platform, not self-hosted, so codec / adaptive-streaming selection belongs to the upstream player rather than to this layer:\n\n  + **Product videos → Vimeo**, referenced by embed URL stored in product data or the CMS (PRD-067). Load behind a lightweight facade (poster image + click/scroll-to-load) so the Vimeo player JS never lands on the LCP critical path.\n  + **Content-managed page videos → Contentful** by default, served from the Contentful asset CDN and referenced within the CMS content entry alongside the page content. Editorial pages may also embed a Vimeo video by URL where preferred (HPC-004) — the product/editorial split above is the expected pattern, not a hard restriction.\n  + Across both: `preload=\"metadata\"` by default; autoplay reserved for muted, short-loop hero clips only.\n* Dynamic imports for heavy non-initial components.\n* Parallel data fetches; no client-side waterfalls. Fetch only required fields.\n\n### 9.3 Where performance is measured\n\nThe CI Lighthouse gate (§14.3) catches regressions but runs against the Dev-tier MRT deployment — slower than production hardware, further from the edge, and not representative of the LCP a real shopper experiences. The signed-off LCP and Lighthouse targets in this section are therefore validated against **Stage**, which mirrors production infrastructure (same MRT bundle size class, same CDN/edge configuration, same SFCC realm tier). Treat CI numbers as regression indicators; treat Stage numbers as the contractual ones.\n\n> **SSR connection pool tuning:** the initial per-pool connection caps defined in §12.1 are derived from expected concurrency, not observed traffic. The first Stage performance run should include per-pool queue-depth and connection-wait metrics (undici emits these as diagnostic channel events). Use those numbers to validate or revise the caps.\n\n---\n\n10. Accessibility\n-----------------\n\n**Target:** WCAG 2.2 AA, with WCAG 2.1 AA as the floor that is always met.\n\n**Automated coverage:**\n\n* axe-core via `vitest-axe` at the unit layer and `axe-playwright` at the E2E layer\n* Storybook a11y addon at component level during development\n* Lighthouse CI accessibility score ≥ 0.90 in the SIT gate\n\n**Manual coverage:** delegated to Zimmermann's existing accessibility vendor (unconfirmed — vendor, screen-reader / browser matrix, and audit cadence to be supplied by Zimmermann).\n\n---\n\n11. Consent & Marketing\n-----------------------\n\n### 11.1 Didomi gating (REG-048–056)\n\n* Cookie consent banner on first visit, all storefronts; preferences persist across sessions and are reviewable/updatable per category at any time.\n* **No third-party tracking or marketing script loads until consent is granted** — including GTM (and the tags Tell No Lies loads downstream: Meta, Pinterest, TikTok, GA4, etc.), the PWA-direct head tags Optimizely Web and Crazy Egg (AAB-004/005), Inside Chat, Narvar widgets.\n* **reCAPTCHA is not a tracking script** (CRM-008). It loads on register / login form interaction only, deferred to keep it off LCP. **Regional split:** in **EU & UK** it is consent-gated and loads only after Didomi consent — with the accepted consequence that a visitor who declines consent is never challenged, so reCAPTCHA provides no bot protection there. In **all other regions** it loads on form interaction regardless of consent state.\n* GTM integrated with Didomi for **Google Consent Mode v2**; hashed email passed at order confirmation for Enhanced Conversions.\n* Where consent state cannot be determined, the system **fails closed** — no third-party scripts load.\n* Per-storefront consent defaults are specified under REG-054.\n\n### 11.2 Marketing opt-in sync\n\n`FE → SFCC → Omneo → Emarsys` is the opt-in sync chain (REG-058, REG-059). Sync failures queue and retry silently — they never block the user flow. Trigger points (guest at order placement, registered at registration and My Account preference updates) are functional detail, out of scope for this TSD.\n\n### 11.3 GDPR touchpoints in the PWA\n\nGDPR fulfilment (DSAR, right-to-erasure, retention, breach reporting per REG-060–067) is handled by the SFCC GDPR cartridge and BM configuration — out of scope for this TSD. PWA-side touchpoints are limited to:\n\n* Localised privacy policy pages, served from Contentful per storefront.\n* The consent UI itself (covered in 11.1).\n* Surfacing the customer-initiated erasure / data-export entry point in My Account where exposed by SFCC.\n\n### 11.4 Didomi consent gate reference\n\nImplementation reference for the PWA: which Didomi purpose / vendor ID gates each third-party script. All IDs are **TBC — none have been received from Zimmermann yet.** Guesses are in brackets to orient the conversation.\n\n> Didomi purpose IDs for the three declared categories (Performance, Analytics, Marketing — REG-051), plus per-environment Notice IDs, are open items (§12.1 / §12.2 of the Open Items register). All vendor-specific IDs below depend on those being provisioned first.\n\n| Integration | Didomi purpose / vendor ID | Scope | Open item ref |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **GTM (Addingwell)** — loads Meta, GA4, Pinterest, TikTok downstream | Handled by Tell No Lies in the GTM container / Addingwell workspace | All storefronts | — |\n| **Inside Chat** | TBC | All storefronts | §14.1 |\n| **Optimizely Web** | TBC (Analytics?) | US storefront only | §15.2 |\n| **Crazy Egg** | TBC (Analytics?) | All storefronts | §16.3 |\n| **Narvar Promise** | TBC (Analytics/Performance? from Narvar/Zimm?) | Domestic storefronts only | §11.2 |\n| **reCAPTCHA v3** | n/a — no consent required; loads on form interaction | All except EU & UK | — |\n| **reCAPTCHA v3** | TBC | EU & UK storefronts only | §10.2 |\n| **Measmerize** | TBC | PDP only | §17.1 |\n| **Vimeo** | TBC (Marketing/Analytics? use `dnt=1` if no consent) | PDP + editorial pages, all storefronts | §18.1 |\n\n---\n\n12. Graceful Degradation\n------------------------\n\nEach external system fails independently — one outage does not break a page.\n\n| System | Failure mode |\n| --- | --- |\n| Contentful | Render page without editorial content; never block commerce data on CMS |\n| Global-e | Surface explicit error; do not silently fall back to SFCC for international checkout |\n| Didomi | Fail closed — no consent, no third-party scripts |\n| Omneo (writes) / Emarsys | Queue and retry silently; never block the user flow |\n| Omneo (reads) | **Load-bearing for My Account profile pages** — per DMU-004 only email + Omneo ID are migrated; all other customer profile data is sourced live from Omneo at login. Surface an explicit retry/error UI on profile pages if the round-trip fails; do not render stale or partial profile data. |\n| Google Maps | Degrade to manual address entry; never hard-block checkout |\n| AP21 (Retail Rewards) | On AP21 unavailability at the point of Rewards application, inform the customer and allow an alternative payment method |\n| Narvar (EDD) | Suppress widget; do not show an error or block checkout |\n\n### 12.1 Blast-Radius Containment\n\nThe failure modes above describe what the PWA does when a single integration is unavailable. This section covers how to limit the *spread* of an attack or outage — ensuring a degraded integration cannot consume shared resources and take down unrelated features.\n\n#### Edge (outside the PWA — Infrastructure / SI Partner)\n\n* **Web Application Firewalls + rate limiting** is the primary lever for volumetric attacks and credential-stuffing.\n* **SFCC Business Manager** provides API-level rate limiting for SFCC endpoints, independent of the storefront edge.\n\n#### SSR layer (inside the PWA)\n\n`overrides/app/ssr.js` makes server-side `fetch` calls directly (token exchange, JWKS, SF Payments metadata). Node's native `fetch` is backed by `undici` internally. Undici's `dispatcher` option (the per-call `dispatcher` option on individual `fetch()` invocations) allows each upstream origin to be given its own connection pool with an independent cap. This prevents a slow or flooded integration (e.g. checkout APIs) from exhausting connections for unrelated origins (e.g. Contentful, JWKS).\n\n> **Implementation note:** the per-call `dispatcher` option is safer than the `setGlobalDispatcher` in a managed environment (MRT) since it does not touch the global dispatcher that Salesforce internal libraries may rely on. The `commerce-sdk-react` hooks manage their own HTTP client server-side (Commerce SDK) and are not affected in either approach.\n\nThe connection pool split is an engineering decision — the right pool boundaries and sizes depend on observed traffic patterns and the integration map in §7. Planned pools (connection caps should be validated later — see §9.3):\n\n| Pool | Origin | Calls | Cap | `connectTimeout` | `bodyTimeout` | Reasoning |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `sfcc-scapi` | `<https://{shortCode}.api.commercecloud.salesforce.com`> | SLAS JWKS (token validation for passwordless login, reset password) | 5 | 5 s | 8 s | `jose` caches the JWK set for its rotation period; actual network calls are rare. Isolated so a JWKS fetch is never starved by higher-volume pools. |\n| `sfcc-payments` | `config.app.sfPayments.metadataUrl` host (a `*.demandware.net` or SCAPI hostname — waiting confirmation from Mani) | Payment method metadata on every checkout page SSR | 10 | 5 s | 8 s | Called on every checkout page load; moderate frequency. Separate from `sfcc-scapi` because a checkout traffic spike must not consume SLAS connections. |\n| `mulesoft` | `MULESOFT_BASE_URL` | Order history, order details, boutique / BOPIS stock check, GiveX balance check | 20 | 5 s | 10 s | Highest user-facing concurrency (My Account + PDP). GiveX balance calls carry payment credentials in the POST body — a slow MuleSoft response must not hold connections open and starve other pools. Highest cap reflects the broadest call surface. |\n| `marketing-cloud-auth` | `<https://{subdomain}.auth.marketingcloudapis.com`> | Marketing Cloud client-credentials token exchange | 5 | 5 s | 15 s | User-triggered, infrequent; token cached at module scope for 15 min. Isolated so an MC outage cannot consume SFCC or MuleSoft connections. |\n| `marketing-cloud-rest` | `<https://{subdomain}.rest.marketingcloudapis.com`> | Magic-link email send (passwordless login, reset password) | 5 | 5 s | 15 s | Same trigger as above; higher body timeout because email delivery APIs can respond slowly under load. |\n| `google-places` | `<https://places.googleapis.com`> (Places API — New) | Address autocomplete at checkout (CFD-023, CFD-030, CFD-059) | 5 | 3 s | 5 s | Non-critical-path; graceful fallback to manual entry. Short timeouts ensure a Places API slowdown releases connections quickly. |\n| `booxi` | Booxi base URL (TBD) | Boutique appointment read in My Account (CRM-165–167) | 5 | 3 s | 5 s | My Account only; non-critical-path within that page. |\n\n**Contentful:** The Contentful SDK (`contentful` v11) does not expose a `dispatcher` hook on its internal fetch calls, so connection isolation for Contentful is not achievable through this pattern. Primary mitigation is the graceful degradation rule in §12 (Render page without editorial content).\n\n---\n\n13. Testing Strategy\n--------------------\n\nThree layers, no duplicated coverage — the cheapest layer that proves a behaviour wins.\n\n| Layer | Tool | Covers |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | Vitest | Pure logic, data mapping, transforms, isolated hooks |\n| Component interaction | Storybook `play` | Single-component user interactions |\n| E2E | Playwright | Full journeys, multi-region, integration boundaries |\n\n### 13.1 What is tested\n\nFailure paths that would be non-obvious or costly to debug: Contentful → component data mapping, locale / currency transforms, Global-e pricing / tax branching, checkout ownership, consent gating, cart mutations, optimistic-update rollback. Framework behaviour and pure visual rendering are not tested at this layer.\n\n### 13.2 E2E gherkin-first workflow\n\nEvery Playwright spec starts as a Gherkin `.feature` file that Lead QA validates in plain English before any code is written. This prevents technically correct tests of the wrong behaviour.\n\n### 13.3 Multi-region fixtures\n\nE2E specs are parameterised by `SITE_ID` from day one — every journey runs across all 8 storefronts and 11 active locales, with site-specific `baseUrl`, currency, checkout owner, tax model, and consent default supplied by a fixture rather than hardcoded. (Because a test hardcoded to one region cannot be cheaply expanded later)\n\n### 13.4 Selector priority\n\nTests select elements the way users and assistive tech perceive them: accessible selectors (role + name, label, visible text) first, semantic HTML attributes second (`alt`, `title`), `data-testid` only when nothing semantic applies. Tag/class/DOM-position selectors are not used. This doubles as a continuous accessibility check.\n\n---\n\n14. CI/CD & Environments\n------------------------\n\nThis section probably belong to a DevOps TSD, but will put here temporarily until we have one.\n\n### 14.1 Topology\n\nA six-tier environment topology spans **Local → Dev → SIT → Stage → UAT → Prod**. Each tier (other than Local) has its own SFCC realm, Contentful environment, and MuleSoft connections. Ownership splits across three zones — Digital, IT/NewStore, and Shared (Digital + Business).\n\n| # | Tier | Owner | Purpose | Third-party availability | Deploy trigger |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Local** | Digital | Feature build on developer machines | All services mocked (MSW stubs) | `npm start` |\n| 2 | **Dev** | Digital | Integrated digital testing across all 6 storefronts, independent of NewStore | MuleSoft STG · NewStore Dev · Omneo STG · Narvar stub · Adyen sandbox | Merge to `develop` |\n| 3 | **SIT** | IT / NewStore | NewStore SIT and regression on its own cadence | NewStore STG · MuleSoft STG · Narvar STG · AP21 test | NewStore release cadence (parallel lane, non-blocking for Digital releases) |\n| 4 | **Stage** (E2E) | Shared | End-to-end testing with all third parties live; time-boxed booked windows | NewStore STG · MuleSoft STG · Omneo STG · Narvar STG · Adyen sandbox | Booked 2-week window |\n| 5 | **UAT** | Shared | Business acceptance across all 6 regions | NewStore STG · Narvar STG · Omneo STG · Adyen sandbox | Business approval gate |\n| 6 | **Prod** | Live | Production; AU canary then flag-flip to remaining regions | NewStore PROD · MuleSoft PROD · Omneo PROD · Narvar PROD · Adyen PROD | Release manager + canary gate |\n\nStage is the **contractual performance-test environment** referenced in §9.3 — it mirrors production infrastructure (same MRT bundle class, CDN/edge configuration, SFCC realm tier).\n\n### 14.2 Branch model\n\nGitFlow with explicit back-merges to keep `develop` aligned with production.\n\n| Branch | Branched from | Merges to |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `feature/*` | `develop` | `develop` |\n| `fix/*` | `develop` | `develop` |\n| `release/*` | `develop` | `main` **and** `develop` |\n| `fix/release-{version}-*` | the `release/*` branch | the `release/*` branch (forward-merged to `develop` via the release back-merge) |\n| `hotfix/*` | `main` | `main` **and** `develop` |\n| `develop`, `main` | — | protected; PR-only |\n\nRelease branches are supervised — code logic fixes go on a `fix/release-{version}-*` sub-branch rather than directly on the release branch. Releases and hotfixes always back-merge to `develop` so production fixes flow forward automatically.\n\n### 14.3 CI workflows\n\n**QA workflow (**`qa.yml`) — runs on every PR to `develop` and `release/**`. Code-correctness checks with no environment dependency:\n\n* Unit tests (Vitest)\n* Storybook interaction tests (`play` functions) and component-level accessibility checks via `@storybook/addon-a11y` (`storybook test --ci`)\n\n**SIT gate (**`digital-sit.yml`) — runs on push/PR to `release/**`; on merge to `develop` it also deploys to the **Dev** environment (tier 2 in §14.1). Environment-dependent checks:\n\n* Playwright `p0` priority specs\n* Bundle-size check against the ceilings in §9\n* Contract tests against the MuleSoft OpenAPI specification\n* Lighthouse CI thresholds (Performance ≥ 0.80, Accessibility ≥ 0.90, Best Practices ≥ 0.90)\n* Trend gate against the LHCI history server\n\n### 14.4 Deployment\n\nDeployment is via Salesforce Managed Runtime. Each tier maps to its own MRT target:\n\n* **Dev** — deployed on merge to `develop`, gated on a clean SIT gate (§14.3)\n* **SIT** — deployed on the NewStore release cadence, isolated from the Digital release path (failures do not block `release/**`)\n* **Stage** — deployed on a booked 2-week window for full end-to-end testing with live third parties\n* **UAT** — deployed for business acceptance; promotion requires Business sign-off\n* **Prod** — release-manager-gated; AU canary first, then flag-flip to remaining regions\n\nProduction deploys are gated on a clean SIT gate run plus stakeholder approval through Stage and UAT.\n\nMRT deployment targets map one-to-one to the topology: `dev | sit | stage | uat | production`.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f39b"
  }
}"}

